From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 07:38:06 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] uml: complete or report a Darwin kernel-range TLB flush

flush_tlb_kernel_range() discarded um_tlb_sync()'s result. On Darwin
flush_cache_vmap() and flush_cache_vunmap() are both that macro, so every
vmalloc map and unmap in the guest went through one call whose failure nobody
observed.

The unmap side cannot be recovered afterwards. __purge_vmap_area_lazy() calls
flush_tlb_kernel_range() immediately before releasing the addresses for reuse,
so a dropped unmap leaves the host serving a page the guest has freed, at an
address the allocator may hand out at once. Nothing ever faults on a mapping
that should not exist, so the lazy repair in segv() cannot notice. A guest
kernel write through such an address lands in whatever took the page over,
which is the "BUG: Bad page map" and "Bad swap file entry" class of damage.

Route the kernel range through um_tlb_sync_kernel_range(), which retries a
transient host failure a bounded number of times and otherwise stops the
kernel rather than returning with the host and init_mm disagreeing. A nested
request -- a kernel-range flush raised from inside the walker that already
owns the turnstile, which um_tlb_sync_init_mm() reports as -EDEADLK -- is
recorded and drained by that walker before it releases ownership, so it is
deferred but never dropped. Draining is keyed on that request alone, not on
um_tlb_sync_pending(), because set_ptes() marks constantly and a
pending-based loop re-walks the whole range on every sync.

A failed init_mm host map now unmaps its own address, so a map failure
degrades to "not mapped", which segv() already repairs, instead of leaving
older backing live under a page table entry that names a different page.

Finally, assert what the wide-unmap arms rely on. Darwin compiles out
pmd_mkuptodate() and friends, so _PAGE_NEEDSYNC is never cleared and a
cleared kernel entry would re-unmap its whole span on every covering walk.
Nothing frees a vmalloc page table, so no init_mm entry reaches those arms --
measured over 38163 kernel-range flushes, where the arms fired 2934 times for
user mms and never for init_mm. WARN_ON_ONCE() keeps that true.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/um/include/asm/tlbflush.h |   5 ++
 arch/um/kernel/tlb.c           | 111 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 115 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/um/include/asm/tlbflush.h b/arch/um/include/asm/tlbflush.h
index bd049ef7a30551999bbafa7b3bfef08932df43d6..458f68c3840a1ef17578329c8470f2fbae2a9cb1 100644
--- a/arch/um/include/asm/tlbflush.h
+++ b/arch/um/include/asm/tlbflush.h
@@ -33,6 +33,7 @@
 extern int um_tlb_sync(struct mm_struct *mm);
 
 #ifdef UML_HOST_DARWIN
+extern void um_tlb_sync_kernel_range(unsigned long start, unsigned long end);
 extern bool um_tlb_sync_pending(struct mm_struct *mm);
 extern int um_tlb_sync_locked(struct mm_struct *mm, unsigned long start,
 			      unsigned long end);
@@ -74,7 +75,11 @@ static inline void flush_tlb_kernel_range(unsigned long start,
 	um_tlb_mark_sync(&init_mm, start, end);
 
 	/* Kernel needs to be synced immediately */
+#ifdef UML_HOST_DARWIN
+	um_tlb_sync_kernel_range(start, end);
+#else
 	um_tlb_sync(&init_mm);
+#endif
 }
 
 #endif
diff --git a/arch/um/kernel/tlb.c b/arch/um/kernel/tlb.c
index aeef0bd64b989e85535fce0cdf67cea1de8a9599..fe69a5b2fc988009bce2481ebeb524dad94a6680 100644
--- a/arch/um/kernel/tlb.c
+++ b/arch/um/kernel/tlb.c
@@ -333,6 +333,15 @@ static inline int update_pte_range(pmd_t *pmd, unsigned long addr,
 
 			ret = ops->mmap(ops->mm_idp, addr, PAGE_SIZE,
 					prot, fd, offset);
+#ifdef UML_HOST_DARWIN
+			/* Nothing ever faults on a kernel mapping that names
+			 * the wrong page, so a failed map must not leave the
+			 * previous backing live here.  Degrade to "not
+			 * mapped", which the repair in segv() owns.
+			 */
+			if (unlikely(ret && ops->mm == &init_mm))
+				(void)ops->unmap(ops->mm_idp, addr, PAGE_SIZE);
+#endif
 		} else
 			ret = ops->unmap(ops->mm_idp, addr, PAGE_SIZE);
 
@@ -362,6 +371,16 @@ static inline int update_pmd_range(pud_t *pud, unsigned long addr,
 		next = pmd_addr_end(addr, end);
 		if (!pmd_present(*pmd)) {
 			if (pmd_needsync(*pmd)) {
+#ifdef UML_HOST_DARWIN
+				/* Darwin never clears _PAGE_NEEDSYNC, so a
+				 * cleared kernel entry would re-unmap this
+				 * whole span on every covering walk.  Nothing
+				 * frees a vmalloc page table, so no init_mm
+				 * entry reaches this arm; assert it rather
+				 * than rely on it silently.
+				 */
+				WARN_ON_ONCE(ops->mm == &init_mm);
+#endif
 				ret = ops->unmap(ops->mm_idp, addr,
 						 next - addr);
 #ifndef UML_HOST_DARWIN
@@ -388,6 +407,16 @@ static inline int update_pud_range(p4d_t *p4d, unsigned long addr,
 		next = pud_addr_end(addr, end);
 		if (!pud_present(*pud)) {
 			if (pud_needsync(*pud)) {
+#ifdef UML_HOST_DARWIN
+				/* Darwin never clears _PAGE_NEEDSYNC, so a
+				 * cleared kernel entry would re-unmap this
+				 * whole span on every covering walk.  Nothing
+				 * frees a vmalloc page table, so no init_mm
+				 * entry reaches this arm; assert it rather
+				 * than rely on it silently.
+				 */
+				WARN_ON_ONCE(ops->mm == &init_mm);
+#endif
 				ret = ops->unmap(ops->mm_idp, addr,
 						 next - addr);
 #ifndef UML_HOST_DARWIN
@@ -413,6 +442,16 @@ static inline int update_p4d_range(pgd_t *pgd, unsigned long addr,
 		next = p4d_addr_end(addr, end);
 		if (!p4d_present(*p4d)) {
 			if (p4d_needsync(*p4d)) {
+#ifdef UML_HOST_DARWIN
+				/* Darwin never clears _PAGE_NEEDSYNC, so a
+				 * cleared kernel entry would re-unmap this
+				 * whole span on every covering walk.  Nothing
+				 * frees a vmalloc page table, so no init_mm
+				 * entry reaches this arm; assert it rather
+				 * than rely on it silently.
+				 */
+				WARN_ON_ONCE(ops->mm == &init_mm);
+#endif
 				ret = ops->unmap(ops->mm_idp, addr,
 						 next - addr);
 #ifndef UML_HOST_DARWIN
@@ -437,6 +476,16 @@ static int update_mm_range(struct mm_struct *mm, struct vm_ops *ops,
 		next = pgd_addr_end(addr, end);
 		if (!pgd_present(*pgd)) {
 			if (pgd_needsync(*pgd)) {
+#ifdef UML_HOST_DARWIN
+				/* Darwin never clears _PAGE_NEEDSYNC, so a
+				 * cleared kernel entry would re-unmap this
+				 * whole span on every covering walk.  Nothing
+				 * frees a vmalloc page table, so no init_mm
+				 * entry reaches this arm; assert it rather
+				 * than rely on it silently.
+				 */
+				WARN_ON_ONCE(ops->mm == &init_mm);
+#endif
 				ret = ops->unmap(ops->mm_idp, addr,
 						 next - addr);
 #ifndef UML_HOST_DARWIN
@@ -674,8 +723,23 @@ static int __um_tlb_sync(struct mm_struct *mm,
 #ifdef UML_HOST_DARWIN
 static struct task_struct *init_mm_tlb_owner;
 
+/*
+ * Raised by um_tlb_sync_kernel_range() when a kernel-range flush is requested
+ * from inside the walker that already owns the turnstile.  The owner drains it
+ * before releasing, so such a request is deferred but never dropped.
+ */
+static bool init_mm_tlb_nested;
+
+/*
+ * A nested request can only come from a host operation the walker itself
+ * performed, so the drain converges.  Bound it anyway: looping forever inside
+ * the kernel would be worse than stopping with a diagnosis.
+ */
+#define UM_TLB_KERNEL_DRAIN_ROUNDS 8
+
 static noinline int um_tlb_sync_init_mm(void)
 {
+	unsigned int round;
 	int ret;
 
 	/* A host fault inside an init_mm host mapping operation re-enters the
@@ -691,7 +755,16 @@ static noinline int um_tlb_sync_init_mm(void)
 		goto clear_owner;
 	}
 	WRITE_ONCE(init_mm_tlb_owner, current);
-	ret = __um_tlb_sync(&init_mm, false, 0, 0);
+	for (round = 0; round < UM_TLB_KERNEL_DRAIN_ROUNDS; round++) {
+		/* Clear before walking so a request raised during the walk is
+		 * seen by the next test rather than lost.
+		 */
+		WRITE_ONCE(init_mm_tlb_nested, false);
+		ret = __um_tlb_sync(&init_mm, false, 0, 0);
+		if (ret || !READ_ONCE(init_mm_tlb_nested))
+			goto clear_owner;
+	}
+	panic("Nested kernel TLB sync did not converge");
 clear_owner:
 	WRITE_ONCE(init_mm_tlb_owner, NULL);
 	mutex_unlock(&init_mm.context.turnstile);
@@ -709,6 +782,42 @@ int um_tlb_sync(struct mm_struct *mm)
 }
 
 #ifdef UML_HOST_DARWIN
+/* A transient host failure is worth retrying; a persistent one is not. */
+#define UM_TLB_KERNEL_SYNC_ATTEMPTS 4
+
+/*
+ * flush_cache_vmap() and flush_cache_vunmap() are both flush_tlb_kernel_range()
+ * on this port, so every vmalloc map and unmap arrives here.  The unmap side
+ * cannot be recovered later: __purge_vmap_area_lazy() releases the address for
+ * reuse immediately afterwards, and nothing ever faults on a mapping that
+ * should not exist, so the lazy repair in segv() never learns of it.  A guest
+ * that returns from this function may assume the host agrees with init_mm's
+ * page tables over the range, or that the kernel has stopped.
+ */
+void um_tlb_sync_kernel_range(unsigned long start, unsigned long end)
+{
+	unsigned int attempt;
+	int ret = 0;
+
+	for (attempt = 0; attempt < UM_TLB_KERNEL_SYNC_ATTEMPTS; attempt++) {
+		ret = um_tlb_sync(&init_mm);
+		if (!ret)
+			return;
+		/* The walker that owns the turnstile is on this task's stack
+		 * and drains the range before it releases ownership, so the
+		 * request is deferred to it rather than dropped.
+		 */
+		if (ret == -EDEADLK) {
+			WRITE_ONCE(init_mm_tlb_nested, true);
+			return;
+		}
+	}
+
+	if (ret == -ENOMEM)
+		report_enomem();
+	panic("Failed to sync kernel TLBs over %#lx-%#lx: %d", start, end, ret);
+}
+
 bool um_tlb_sync_pending(struct mm_struct *mm)
 {
 	unsigned long flags;
